<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sumer</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sumerian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sumer was the region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> where one of the world's earliest civilisations developed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>. It was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> describe the revival of Sumerian power under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sumer.jpg" alt="Sumer" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA Overlay: पाटलिपुत्र · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sumer_satellite_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to tradition, Sargon began as cupbearer to King Ur-Zababa of Kish, a position that gave him access to the court and administration of one of <b>Sumer</b>'s leading city-states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its merchants carried copper from Magan, carnelian, ivory and other luxuries from Meluhha, the Indus region, and pearls, called “fish-eyes” in Mesopotamian texts, while they brought back barley, wool, textiles, oil and silver from <b>Sumer</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1974 and 1975 the excavators found the archives of Palace G, the royal palace of the twenty-fourth century, which transformed knowledge of early Syrian history by showing that a literate, centralised state had flourished there alongside the city-states of <b>Sumer</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The wars of Lagash are among the best recorded of early <b>Sumer</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Ur III period hymns praising rulers became a popular genre, and a poem describes the death of Ur-Nammu and his journey to the underworld; at that period's end the Lament for <b>Sumer</b> and Ur and the Lament for Ur recorded the city's fall.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The greatest moment came when Lugal-zage-si, ruler of Umma, defeated Lagash, took Uruk as his capital and claimed rule over all <b>Sumer</b>, from the Persian Gulf to the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Military</a></li></ul></section>
